+\paragraph{System isomorphism and good-place overlaps (Block 25).}
+The system equivalence used here is componentwise representation isomorphism:
+\[
+ \mathscr W_i\simeq\mathscr W_j
+ \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
+ \forall\lambda,\ W_{i,\lambda}\simeq W_{j,\lambda}.
+\]
+For finite-dimensional irreducible representations of any monoid over a
+characteristic-zero field, the new character-rigidity theorem proves that
+equality of characters is equivalent to an actual equivariant linear isomorphism.
+Neither a finite group nor an algebraically closed coefficient field is needed.
+
+Suppose two members are isomorphic at $\lambda_0$. Injectivity of the common
+coefficient embedding gives $t_i(v)=t_j(v)$ at every place good for $\lambda_0$.
+At another coefficient prime $\lambda$, this equality compares actual member
+traces on the intersection of the two good-place sets. Conjugacy density of
+that intersection and continuity extend the equality to the whole group;
+character rigidity then gives an actual isomorphism at $\lambda$.
+Thus the nonisomorphism of systems supplies the required nonisomorphism of
+members. Equality of optional labels or record fields is not used as a
+substitute for representation equivalence.
+
+The generic separator now derives this bridge and permits any additional finite
+set of excluded places. Its coverage premise retains every finite deletion:
+for each finite excluded set and each open normal quotient, every quotient
+class contains a conjugate of an allowed sample. This implies density on each
+pair of cofinite good-place sets; no all-coefficient-prime common ordinary-trace
+domain is assumed.
+
+\paragraph{Arithmetic coverage for a fixed cyclotomic extension (Block 25).}
+Let $n>0$ and let $L=\Q(\zeta_n)$ be a cyclotomic number field. The canonical
+isomorphism
+\[
+ \operatorname{Gal}(L/\Q)\simeq(\mathbb Z/n\mathbb Z)^\times
+\]
+defines $\sigma_p$ by $\sigma_p(\zeta_n)=\zeta_n^p$ when $p\nmid n$.
+For every automorphism $\sigma$, finite set $B\subset\mathbb N$ and bound $b$,
+Lean proves that there is a rational prime $p>b$, with $p\notin B$ and
+$p\nmid n$, such that $\sigma_p=\sigma$. The prime-existence step uses the
+proved Dirichlet theorem in the pinned Mathlib dependency.
+
+The arithmetic attachment is checked in this finite cyclotomic case. There is
+an actual maximal prime $\mathfrak P$ of $\mathcal O_L$ above $(p)$, its
+ramification index is one, and $\sigma_p$ belongs to its decomposition group.
+For every integral $x\in\mathcal O_L$,
+\[
+ \overline{\sigma_p(x)}=\overline{x}^{\,p}
+ \quad\text{in }\mathcal O_L/\mathfrak P.
+\]
+The residue identity is proved on all integral elements using
+$\mathcal O_L=\mathbb Z[\zeta_n]$. Existence of $\mathfrak P$ is obtained by
+lying over, rather than assumed. This specifies arithmetic Frobenius, with
+$p$-power residue action. The action and unramifiedness statements also hold
+for every maximal prime above the selected $p$.
+
+Consequently, for pairwise componentwise nonisomorphic compatible irreducible
+representation families of this fixed finite Galois group, the common algebraic
+trace matrix can be selected at distinct rational primes outside any prescribed
+finite set, with nonzero determinant. Every coefficient embedding preserves its
+nonvanishing; actual member trace comparisons retain that member's good-prime
+condition. No coverage or character-continuity hypothesis is added in this
+cyclotomic specialization: the prime coverage is proved, and the finite
+extension's Krull topology is discrete. A concrete conductor-four instance uses
+the actual field $\Q(\zeta_4)$.
+
+This is a complete restricted arithmetic case of Claim 9.1. It does not establish
+coverage in arbitrary finite quotients of the absolute Galois group, nor the
+Hodge--Tate and Weil--Deligne comparisons of Claim 9.2. The source-claim ledger
+therefore remains seventeen checked and three partial.
+
+\paragraph{Finite deletion can destroy density.}
+In the actual discrete group $C_2=\{1,a\}$, set $s(0)=a$ and $s(m+1)=1$.
+The original samples are surjective and satisfy quotient coverage. Removing
+index zero leaves only the identity, so both density and quotient coverage fail.
+The actual rational sign and trivial characters agree on the remaining samples
+but differ globally. This checked example explains why ordinary coverage alone
+cannot justify deleting the bad places in the system comparison.
 
